      "dom_id": "question-44574",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $2{x}^{2}+4x+k=0$ has two distinct real solutions.",
      "worked_solution": "The equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is positive.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=2,$ $b=4$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(4)^{2}-4(2)(k) \\\\  & =16-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & >0 \\\\ 16-8k & >0 \\\\ 16 & >8k \\\\ 8k & <16 \\\\ k & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have two distinct real solutions if $k<2.$",
