      "problem": "Consider the equation $-{x}^{2}-2x+7=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?",
      "worked_solution": "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(-1)(7) \\\\  & =4+28 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has two distinct real solutions.",
